  "Number": 2,
  "Question": "A box contains a collection of triangular and square tiles. There are \\(25\\) tiles in the box, containing \\(84\\) edges total. How many square tiles are there in the box?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(3\\)",
    "B": "\\(5\\)",
    "C": "\\(7\\)",
    "D": "\\(9\\)",
    "E": "\\(11\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "A triangle has \\(3\\) edges and a square has \\(4\\), so a square contributes exactly one more edge than a triangle. If all \\(25\\) tiles were triangles, there would be\n\\[\n25\\cdot3=75\n\\]\nedges. Replacing a triangle with a square keeps the number of tiles unchanged and adds exactly one edge.\n\nThe actual total has \\(84-75=9\\) extra edges, so exactly \\(9\\) tiles must be squares. This leaves \\(16\\) triangles, and \\(16\\cdot3+9\\cdot4=84\\), as required. There are \\(\\boxed{9}\\) square tiles, choice D.",
